  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nCurrently a large upper level high is in the Central Plains \nwith a ridge building in the Pacific Northwest. In the Northern \nPlains quasi-zonal flow with a slight northwest tilt is present.\nA small upper level low is about to dig through the Lake \nSuperior region, with a trailing cold front in southern Canada \nto its west. At the surface this cold is present too, currently \nnearing the North Dakota border. Ahead of the front is low \ncumulus clouds in northern North Dakota, and breezy northwest \nwinds across the state from the cold front last night. This \nevening that cold front might kick off scattered showers and \nmaybe a thunderstorm in the north and central. With almost no \nCAPE but 40kts of shear, a brief strong storm is possible with \nsmall hail.\n\nTuesday will be the coolest day of the forecast period with\nhighs slightly below normal in the 70s and 80s as the flow turns\nmore northwest before the incoming ridge. The rest of this week\nhowever the strong warming trend will start once again. The\ncurrent ridge in the Pacific Northwest will slide east to the\nNorthern Plains with the ridge axis ending up over the western\npart of the state on Saturday. A strong thermal ridge will\nfollow with temperatures at 850mb nearing 100 degrees over the\nstate on Saturday, with the compressional heating of that air \nmixing down, the current NBM highs this upcoming weekend around \n105 in the southwest and south central. Looking at the NBM \npercentiles, these deterministic values are actually only the \n50th percentile. The 90th temperatures are close to 110 in the \nsouth. It is very possible that the NBM currently has a warm \nbias because we have been so far above normal in the last few \nweeks, and this incoming ridge actually looks a little weaker \nand less humid than the last one. Only time will tell on what \nhigh temperatures actually happen. Saturday the ridge breaks \ndown back to near zonal flow, leading to slight chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms Saturday with the flow off the \nmountains. The rest of the weekend will continue to be very \nwarm. Heat products will likely be needed again.",
